// @reject: decision 15, with an element the analyser cannot type. `p` is a
// @reject: parameter, so nothing says what it holds — but `9` and "x" are
// @reject: literals, and they already disagree. The two Rust engines compare
// @reject: each element with the FIRST and treat an untyped one as compatible
// @reject: with anything, so they refuse this ("array element 2 has type
// @reject: String"). The browser engine compared only when it knew every
// @reject: element's type: one untyped element switched the check off for the
// @reject: whole literal, and this ran as `[9, x, 1]`. A remainder of DM-04,
// @reject: found debugging GO on 2026-09-27 (GO/Depurando_GO.md DG-05).
f(p) { <~ [9, "x", p] }
>> f(1) ¶
